# Localize — speak like a person

When writing or editing `appLocale` / `clinicLocale` strings (`app/src/i18n/*Copy.ts`, portal catalogs, wizard copy):

## Do

- Keep **intent**, change **rhythm**. Same job as English; natural phrasing for that language.
- Prefer short UI beats: **action + short payoff** (especially Hebrew/Arabic).
- Match existing catalog labels for the same action (`logMeal`, `addActivity`, Your setup titles) — don’t invent a parallel verb.
- For RTL (he/ar): write **plain local prose**. Prefer “חיישן סוכר” / “האפליקציה” over mid-sentence Latin when a clear local word exists.

## Don’t

- Don’t translate English **word order** (“From this habit, the rest of the app starts working for you” → awkward Hebrew).
- Don’t pack product thesis into one abstract sentence. CTA copy is not a press release.
- Don’t mix Latin brand/acronyms mid-RTL sentence when it hurts reading (RTL/LTR flicker). Glossary English still belongs on **dedicated labels** and always-English tokens per `language-policy.mdc` (units, slash commands, device rows) — not sprinkled into running Hebrew prose.

## Hebrew smell test

If it sounds like translated English, rewrite.

```
❌ מההרגל הזה שאר האפליקציה מתחילה לעבוד בשבילכם
✅ תעדו ארוחה אחת או פעילות אחת — ומשם הכול מתחבר
```

## Checklist

- [ ] Would a native speaker say this on a phone button / tip card?
- [ ] Same CTA verb as Food Log / Activity / Your setup where applicable?
- [ ] RTL strings free of unnecessary Latin mid-sentence?
- [ ] Glossary English only where language-policy requires it?
